WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.154 --> 00:00:02.802
-Our next guest is a talented
actress you know from films like

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.604
"Girls Trip"
and "Support the Girls,"

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:06.272
as well as the show
"Black Monday."

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.207
She stars in
"Nine Perfect Strangers,"

00:00:08.207 --> 00:00:10.677
which streams new episodes
every Wednesday on Hulu.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:11.810
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:13.946
-I came here to know you.

00:00:13.946 --> 00:00:21.020
To -- To -- To see what he saw,
to see the what and the why.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:25.558
The why...

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:28.094
-Finish that.

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:31.998
-Well, it's just that you -- you
were the catalyst for it all.

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:36.268
You -- You opened his mind
to the idea

00:00:36.268 --> 00:00:39.605
that there were beautiful girls
out there to be had.

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:42.074
-Please welcome back to the show
Regina Hall.

00:00:42.074 --> 00:00:45.611
♪♪

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:48.213
-Hello.

00:00:48.213 --> 00:00:49.715
-Hi.
-Hi.

00:00:49.715 --> 00:00:53.920
-Oh, I can touch you.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.755
Thank you so much.

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.357
♪♪

00:00:58.357 --> 00:01:00.793
-How are you?
-I'm so good.

00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:02.261
-This is so nice for us.

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:04.230
This is some unfinished
business.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:08.534
You and John Krasinski were
our guests March 12, 2020,

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:12.037
the cancelled show where
the world turned upside down.

00:01:12.037 --> 00:01:13.940
And it's so lovely
to finally have you back.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:15.708
-I thought you canceled
because of John.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:17.710
I said, "Oh, he doesn't
want to see John."

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:19.178
-Yeah.

00:01:19.178 --> 00:01:20.412
That would have been
a hard thing to do

00:01:20.412 --> 00:01:22.414
because I really didn't
want to see him

00:01:22.414 --> 00:01:23.750
but I really wanted to see you.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.885
So that day was -- You know,

00:01:25.885 --> 00:01:28.721
COVID actually kind of
did me a favor.

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:29.823
It got me out of
an awkward situation.

00:01:29.823 --> 00:01:31.090
-Exactly.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.092
-We've talked about
your many years in New York.

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:35.027
Went to NYU.
-Yes.

00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:37.330
-You taught at
College of New Rochelle.

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:39.165
-Yes.
-You lived in an apartment

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:40.600
where a crime had recently
been committed?

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.768
-Yes, my roommate and I
went to move in,

00:01:42.768 --> 00:01:44.671
and there was yellow tape.
-Right.

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:46.472
-You know how
when people are murdered,

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:47.940
so I'm assuming
there was a body.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.675
-Right.

00:01:49.675 --> 00:01:52.178
They didn't have the tape
outline though of the body?

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:54.647
-They had the tape --
No, there was tape outline.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.182
-No, but of, like, a body,
though?

00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:57.116
You know the thing
where a body --

00:01:57.116 --> 00:01:58.417
-Well, I don't know.
-Gotcha.

00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:00.820
-I mean, when it's
on the floor, it's --

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:01.954
I think that's what it is.

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:03.156
-Yeah, I think you're
probably right.

00:02:03.156 --> 00:02:04.791
Did you get a deal on the place?

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:06.926
-We left. We went to
another place up the street.

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:09.729
We couldn't stay there.
Okay, we stayed like two weeks.

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:11.063
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:14.334
And then -- We did get our money
back, our full deposit.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:15.668
-Oh, that's good.
-Yeah.

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:17.203
-Because you've heard
horror stories.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:18.371
I mean, though I guess
the real horror story

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:21.841
is the person
who lived there before.

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:23.609
What about --

00:02:23.609 --> 00:02:25.344
In New York, do you like coming
back to New York though?

00:02:25.344 --> 00:02:27.713
Do you have fond memories of it
other than that?

00:02:27.713 --> 00:02:31.350
-I love New York.
I loved New York.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.019
I went to bartending school
in New York.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:34.954
-Yes, at Colombia, yes?
-At Colombia.

00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:37.323
-And yet did you then use
those skills?

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:39.025
-I don't remember anything.

00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:40.226
I was drunk every time I left.

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:41.560
-Really?
-Yes.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.464
-So bartending school,
they let you drink the drinks?

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.699
-You're supposed to spit it out,
I think.

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:49.435
But I don't -- I just --
That felt gross.

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:52.071
And so I swall--
Well, that doesn't sound good.

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:54.540
[ Laughter ]

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.242
-Out of the frying pan
and into the fire.

00:02:57.242 --> 00:03:01.447
-So I drank it.

00:03:01.447 --> 00:03:03.849
And I don't remember anything

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:06.919
except that someone made
good drinks in that class.

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:09.555
-Do you think to this day
you remember anything from it?

00:03:09.555 --> 00:03:11.357
Like, could you make a drink?

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:12.792
-No.
-Okay.

00:03:12.792 --> 00:03:15.527
-The only thing I can make is
peach Schnapps and orange juice.

00:03:15.527 --> 00:03:17.563
Peach Schnapps.
And I did that before I was --

00:03:17.563 --> 00:03:20.132
-Yeah. You know, it's
really never that impressive

00:03:20.132 --> 00:03:22.435
when the recipe is the title.

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:24.069
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:26.339
"How do you make peach Schnapps
and orange juice?"

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:27.673
"Well, you need two things."

00:03:27.673 --> 00:03:30.376
-I can make --
Well, a Bailey's on the rocks.

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.444
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.444 --> 00:03:34.246
-You're moving in
the wrong direction.

00:03:34.246 --> 00:03:36.082
I can do glass.

00:03:36.082 --> 00:03:38.150
If you need a glass,
I can do it right down.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:39.652
-I know. It's so true.

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:42.087
No, I can't make anything.

00:03:42.087 --> 00:03:44.791
-This is a really cool show
with a lot of people that

00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:47.059
you got to shoot in Australia.
-Yes.

00:03:47.059 --> 00:03:49.028
-And we had one of your co-stars
here, Michael Shannon.

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:50.129
-I love Michael.
-We were talking to him.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.764
He is a wonderful,
wonderful gentleman.

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:53.132
-He gave me the finger
all the time.

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.567
-He gave you the finger
all the time?

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.035
-It made me smile.
-Lovingly?

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:57.470
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:59.905
-But he did it slowly,
so it wasn't like a finger.

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:03.242
It would just go up.
He'd say, "Regina?"

00:04:03.242 --> 00:04:05.178
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.178 --> 00:04:07.947
I feel like this is
how he gets you.

00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:10.816
But I kind of picture Michael
Shannon doing everything slowly.

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:12.518
-[ Laughs ]
-You know?

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:15.788
If you told me like the thing
he's doing really fast kicks,

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:18.924
I'd be surprised.
-Trigger finger.

00:04:18.924 --> 00:04:20.493
No. But I loved him.

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:23.929
Michael was like such --
He's like -- I love --

00:04:23.929 --> 00:04:25.932
We had the same eating times,
I think.

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:27.800
-Okay, like you just not --

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.535
Like they didn't assign you
eating times?

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.770
-No. So when we were
in quarantine for two weeks

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.139
at the hotel, this was Michael's
room and this was mine.

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:35.608
So we were right next door
to each other.

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:37.343
And literally the door
would open --

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:39.011
He's the only person I saw
during quarantine.

00:04:39.011 --> 00:04:40.212
-Really?
-The door would open

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:42.715
at the same time,
and then one day I was like --

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:46.652
But it was awkward, you know?
Do you close it?

00:04:46.652 --> 00:04:48.454
-Right, especially when you're
talking about quarantine.

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:50.589
-Yes, and there was a guard
on the floor.

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:53.259
-Had you met him before?
Was this the first time?

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:55.561
-I met him at the --

00:04:55.561 --> 00:04:58.597
I met him once we landed at
the airport, masked.

00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:00.166
-And you had a good group.

00:05:00.166 --> 00:05:01.400
Melissa McCarthy was here
talking about it.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.470
-Melissa McCarthy. She's great.
-Bobby Cannavale.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.905
-But Bobby I had worked with.

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:07.273
-Okay.
-Yes.

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:10.909
Melissa I met at a TIFF party,

00:05:10.909 --> 00:05:12.478
and I told her
we look like twins.

00:05:12.478 --> 00:05:14.079
-Really? Did she buy it?

00:05:14.079 --> 00:05:15.181
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:16.882
-No, but yes.
-What did you guys --

00:05:16.882 --> 00:05:19.117
Did you ever have any fun,
like, off-set stuff?

00:05:19.117 --> 00:05:21.287
Because again, you were probably
limited by your ability

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:23.823
to go out and explore Australia.

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:25.391
-We were, but we did, yeah.

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:26.725
We played cards.

00:05:26.725 --> 00:05:28.261
I was really --

00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:31.664
No. I wasn't good, but when
I came close to winning,

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:33.299
I got so excited.

00:05:33.299 --> 00:05:36.201
-Competitive?
You're competitive?

00:05:36.201 --> 00:05:37.837
-I think so.
-Okay.

00:05:37.837 --> 00:05:39.739
-But not really, because
I'm not good enough --

00:05:39.739 --> 00:05:41.374
You know, to be competitive,
you've got to be really good.

00:05:41.374 --> 00:05:43.109
-That's true. Otherwise you're
just delusional.

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:44.377
-I was delusional.

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:45.812
-What kind of cards
would you play?

00:05:45.812 --> 00:05:48.480
-Some game that Molly taught us.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:49.782
I wish I could think of
the name of it,

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:51.350
but it was like some weird game.

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.318
-It's definitely coming across
that you're not --

00:05:53.318 --> 00:05:54.486
Did you go to card school

00:05:54.486 --> 00:05:55.955
when you were going to
bartending school?

00:05:55.955 --> 00:05:59.258
-I am really good at -- I know.
[ Laughs ]

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:00.926
I'm really good at spades.

00:06:00.926 --> 00:06:01.994
-Oh, yeah. That's a great game.

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:03.829
-Yeah. Can you play?
-Yeah, okay.

00:06:03.829 --> 00:06:06.565
-I think with a little bit of
a primer, I could play spades.

00:06:06.565 --> 00:06:08.801
I play hea-- I was a hearts fan.
-I'm competitive in spades.

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:09.835
-Really?
-Yes.

00:06:09.835 --> 00:06:11.737
-When did you first
start playing?

00:06:11.737 --> 00:06:14.039
Hearts was our,
like, family game.

00:06:14.039 --> 00:06:15.507
-Oh, that's a good one.
-Yeah.

00:06:15.507 --> 00:06:16.909
When did you start playing
spades?

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:18.711
-Oh, I don't even remember
when I didn't play.

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:20.579
-Really? It's your whole life.

00:06:20.579 --> 00:06:23.148
-It's my whole like,
since I was a little girl.

00:06:23.148 --> 00:06:25.718
-This is a show about people,
"Nine Perfect Strangers."

00:06:25.718 --> 00:06:28.454
Or nine -- What is it? It is
"Nine Perfect Strangers," yeah.

00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:30.522
-I've had every name.
My friends have written,

00:06:30.522 --> 00:06:32.425
"I'm loving
'Seven Perfect Strangers.'"

00:06:32.425 --> 00:06:33.926
"I'm loving 'Nine People.'"

00:06:33.926 --> 00:06:36.595
I mean, I've heard -- It's been
called everything on my --

00:06:36.595 --> 00:06:39.665
-"Nine People" is the worst name
for a show.

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:42.301
"Nine People" is like,
"we forgot to give it a name."

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:44.403
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:45.738
"I'm loving 'Nine People.'"

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:48.073
-In real life, because Michael
was talking about

00:06:48.073 --> 00:06:52.277
how he couldn't see himself,
like, doing a yoga retreat.

00:06:52.277 --> 00:06:53.412
Like, that's not for him.

00:06:53.412 --> 00:06:55.348
Have you ever thought of
yourself as somebody

00:06:55.348 --> 00:06:56.549
who could fit in
a wellness retreat?

00:06:56.549 --> 00:06:57.850
-I've gone to wellness retreats.
-You have?

00:06:57.850 --> 00:06:59.519
-Yeah.
-How was it?

00:06:59.519 --> 00:07:00.852
-Well, I've gone to several.

00:07:00.852 --> 00:07:04.623
But actually, my makeup artist,
who is here, Rhiann,

00:07:04.623 --> 00:07:07.427
we went to one together,
which was great.

00:07:07.427 --> 00:07:10.963
Very strict.
We did classes all day.

00:07:10.963 --> 00:07:14.033
You had to --
We juiced three days a week.

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:18.638
You had no cellphones on campus.

00:07:18.638 --> 00:07:21.974
Morning lymph node exercises
to make them drain.

00:07:21.974 --> 00:07:24.743
-What is a lymph node exercise?

00:07:24.743 --> 00:07:27.713
-Jumping to get your lymph nodes
to drain.

00:07:27.713 --> 00:07:30.015
We drank a certain number of
ounces of wheatgrass.

00:07:30.015 --> 00:07:32.317
-Can I jump in? Because I'm
a little bit of a skeptic

00:07:32.317 --> 00:07:34.219
when it comes to stuff.
-Okay.

00:07:34.219 --> 00:07:37.456
If they said, "we're going to do
lymph node exercises,"

00:07:37.456 --> 00:07:38.790
and then I said, "Oh, great,
what is it?"

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:40.926
And they said jumping, I would
be like, "Okay, guys."

00:07:40.926 --> 00:07:42.194
[ Laughter ]
-No, you know,

00:07:42.194 --> 00:07:44.196
jumping on a trampoline
is actually really good

00:07:44.196 --> 00:07:45.898
for your lymph nodes.
-Yeah, that's fine,

00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:47.599
but then it's just jumping
on a trampoline.

00:07:47.599 --> 00:07:49.835
Like, when they call it
a lymph node exercise

00:07:49.835 --> 00:07:51.837
and it's something
that's already --

00:07:51.837 --> 00:07:53.872
-You do a walk
and then there are certain --

00:07:53.872 --> 00:07:56.075
I don't know -- movements that
are supposed to be great

00:07:56.075 --> 00:07:58.443
for the lymph nodes.
-Okay. Now, how long was it?

00:07:58.443 --> 00:08:01.012
A week? What's the longest
wellness retreat you've done?

00:08:01.012 --> 00:08:02.447
-That one was a week.

00:08:02.447 --> 00:08:05.417
You do seven days,
the whole seven days.

00:08:05.417 --> 00:08:07.419
You drink four ounces of
wheatgrass

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:11.257
and you have to do a wheatgrass
enema every day.

00:08:11.257 --> 00:08:12.524
-Really?
-Every day.

00:08:12.524 --> 00:08:15.861
-How is that?

00:08:15.861 --> 00:08:18.163
-What's interesting is not --

00:08:18.163 --> 00:08:20.299
-Every part of it is going to be
interesting to me.

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:22.735
-Not everyone can hold
their wheatgrass.

00:08:22.735 --> 00:08:26.838
-Really?
-Yes.

00:08:26.838 --> 00:08:28.840
In that way.
-Okay.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:33.346
-And the longer you can hold
your wheatgrass,

00:08:33.346 --> 00:08:35.347
the more apparently
it has a chance

00:08:35.347 --> 00:08:39.085
to help heal your intestines.
-Oh, interesting.

00:08:39.085 --> 00:08:41.654
-So you want to hold it up there
as long as possible.

00:08:41.654 --> 00:08:43.489
-Interesting.
-And I --

00:08:43.489 --> 00:08:45.857
I don't even want to brag,
but I must.

00:08:45.857 --> 00:08:49.896
I was told that, um...

00:08:49.896 --> 00:08:52.164
Well, I could hold mine
for a long time.

00:08:52.164 --> 00:08:54.634
-Congratulations.
-Yeah. So...

00:08:54.634 --> 00:08:57.402
-That's not really probably a
skill you'll need anywhere else.

00:08:57.402 --> 00:08:59.805
[ Laughter ]
-Okay, okay.

00:08:59.805 --> 00:09:03.208
I thought it was helping me
for the future.

00:09:03.208 --> 00:09:06.379
-When it's over,
the week-long wellness retreat,

00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:08.080
do you kind of feel like,
"Look at me"?

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:09.281
-You do.
-Really?

00:09:09.281 --> 00:09:10.983
-I glowed.
-You glowed?

00:09:10.983 --> 00:09:15.388
And how many days -- Not that
I'm accusing you of any kind of

00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:17.990
hard living, but how many days
out of the wellness retreat

00:09:17.990 --> 00:09:20.560
do you all of a sudden feel like
yourself before you went in?

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:26.265
-Say it again.

00:09:26.265 --> 00:09:29.935
-Like, I live well for a week
and then, like, is it three days

00:09:29.935 --> 00:09:32.538
of pizza before I'm, like, back
to what I was before I went in?

00:09:32.538 --> 00:09:34.006
-I don't know
because you go out --

00:09:34.006 --> 00:09:36.074
and I will say,
you try to keep the --

00:09:36.074 --> 00:09:37.609
-Yes. You're incentivized.

00:09:37.609 --> 00:09:41.680
-Yes, and so you keep --
Yeah, there's usually a glow.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:43.416
-Did you have a moment where you
left where you were like,

00:09:43.416 --> 00:09:45.117
"I think I'm still holding
my wheatgrass"?

00:09:45.117 --> 00:09:48.421
Or did you make sure --
[ Laughter ]

00:09:48.421 --> 00:09:51.624
-No. But it's --
You know, I have to tell you,

00:09:51.624 --> 00:09:55.060
it's an interesting --
it's an interesting gift

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.828
that I feel like I have.

00:09:56.828 --> 00:09:59.131
-Well, I -- You know, look, I'm
just happy we're giving it out

00:09:59.131 --> 00:10:01.233
to the world,
and now they all know.

00:10:01.233 --> 00:10:03.635
-And now they all know.
-It's so nice to see you.

00:10:03.635 --> 00:10:05.404
Thank you for making
a return trip.

00:10:05.404 --> 00:10:07.239
It has been far too long.
-It was.

00:10:07.239 --> 00:10:09.341
-It speaks to exactly
how difficult

00:10:09.341 --> 00:10:12.644
this last year and a half plus
has been that it took this long

00:10:12.644 --> 00:10:14.246
to have you back,
but it's really worth the wait.

00:10:14.246 --> 00:10:16.815
-And one announcement?
-Yeah?

00:10:16.815 --> 00:10:18.550
-I, too, am having a baby.

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:20.219
-Are you really?
-No. No. No.

00:10:20.219 --> 00:10:21.753
[ Laughter ]
I'm not.

00:10:21.753 --> 00:10:25.257
I just got so excited for John
and Olivia that I was --

00:10:25.257 --> 00:10:26.525
because I couldn't think
of a dad.

00:10:26.525 --> 00:10:28.494
I was going to name you,
but then it's scandalous.

00:10:28.494 --> 00:10:30.863
I was going to name John,
and then it's scandalous.

00:10:30.863 --> 00:10:32.964
I guess I could have named --
-Yeah, Shoemaker.

00:10:32.964 --> 00:10:35.101
-Yeah, Shoemaker.
-There you go. Oh, my God.

00:10:35.101 --> 00:10:36.669
-Are you married?
-Yeah.

00:10:36.669 --> 00:10:38.203
-Well, then that's
kind of scandalous.

00:10:38.203 --> 00:10:41.340
-She won't mind.
[ Laughter ]

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.575
New episodes of
"Nine Perfect Strangers"

00:10:43.575 --> 00:10:44.777
stream Wednesdays on Hulu.

